  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 313 AM CDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nSeasonably strong west-southwesterly mid and upper-level flow \nwill overspread much of the Midwest into the Great Lakes tonight\ninto Wednesday as a large upper trough slides eastward across \nthe north central CONUS. As it does, a north-northwest to south-\nsouthwest oriented surface cold front will gradually shift \nsoutheast from the Upper Midwest today, into the Great Lakes on \nWednesday. This front will be the focus for a few rounds of \nshowers and thunderstorms through the midweek period as forcing \nis augmented by several smaller scale impulses tracking east- \nnorthwestward along it. The threat for a few strong to severe \nstorms cannot be ruled out in our area, particularly on \nWednesday as the front shifts into IL and IN. However, \npotentially of more concern is the threat for an episode or two \nof very heavy rain producing showers and storms in and near our \narea given the axis of deep rich moisture pooling along the \nfront (precipitable water values near 2\").\n\nThere continues to be uncertainty with the exact timing of \nthese waves of showers and storms in our area, owing largely to \nthe timing of, and extent of convective enhancement to the \nindividual smaller scale impulses translating through the larger\nscale trough. However, there continues to be a signal for \nconvection to develop with one such wave near the frontal \nboundary later today into this evening west of our area across \nIA. This activity may then shift east-northeastward into parts \nof northern IL tonight into early Wednesday morning, though \nlikely in a weakening state. Nevertheless, lingering cloud cover\nand rain showers Wednesday morning over parts of northern IL \ncould keep afternoon heating/destabilization tampered. \nAccordingly, this could potentially impact the favored placement\nfor the next round of convection.\n\nAnother round of developing convection is probable into \nWednesday evening/night in association with another impulse \nforecast to sweep eastward across the Mississippi Valley into \nthe lower Great Lakes. If cloud cover and lingering rain from \nany early morning convection linger through the afternoon and \ntamper destabilization enough, it remains plausible that this \nnext round of convection could become favored farther south \n(potentially south of I-88), though how far south this may be is\nunclear at this time. Nevertheless, wherever this evening round\nof showers and thunderstorms sets up, very heavy rainfall and \npossible flash flooding will be a concern, particularly if \nstorms train northeastward over the same areas along and north \nof the frontal boundary.\n\nWhile the Wednesday night convection could push the effective \nfront south of the area into Thursday, forecast guidance \nsuggests that another impulse will track into the region \nThursday afternoon. If this wave is strong enough, it could \nresult in the front lifting back northward and stalling over \nportions of my CWA. If this occurs, an additional wave of heavy \nrain producing convection would be possible Thursday afternoon \nand evening. Confidence on this is low at this time, however.\n\nMainly dry and seasonably warm weather is expected late in the \nweek through potentially most of the upcoming weekend. However, \nensemble guidance favors the Hudson Bay low/trough to remain a \ndominant feature through the weekend before ejecting south-\nsoutheastward into the upper Great Upper Lakes early next week. \nAccordingly, the presence of broad upper troughing across the \nGreat Lakes amid subtropical ridging across the southwestern \nCONUS will support a belt of strong northwesterly mid and upper-\nlevel flow atop another southward shifting surface cold frontal \nboundary early next week. Building instability in the vicinity \nof a boundary will thus support increasing chances for \nconvective episodes (some possible severe with more heavy rain) \nin the area early next week.\n\nKJB",
